From 31d5640cf661cc7df67de5b51c0dfb29e17691c8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Stringer <jcec_1@yahoo.co.uk>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 13:40:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 33/39] Disk write-back: read a track's sector IDs only inside
 its block

An extended track may claim 30-61 sectors in a 256-byte track block (the loader
accepts it). The write-back read that many sector IDs from the track header,
up to 248 bytes past the block, and past the end of the file for the last
track. The ID count is now limited to what fits in the block; such a track
then differs from the drive's copy and is written from it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/cpc/diskimage/dskwrite.c | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/cpc/diskimage/dskwrite.c b/src/cpc/diskimage/dskwrite.c
index e84aa4d..95e77a9 100644
--- a/src/cpc/diskimage/dskwrite.c
+++ b/src/cpc/diskimage/dskwrite.c
@@ -81,6 +81,11 @@ static int parse_image(const unsigned char *d, unsigned long len, WB_IMAGE *im)
 		spt = th[0x15];
 		if (im->ext) { if (spt > 29) { if (spt > 61) spt = 61; p = off + 512; } else p = off + 256; }
 		else { if (spt > 29) spt = 29; p = off + 256; }
+		/* the ID table must lie inside this track's block (an extended track with
+		 * 30-61 IDs in a 256-byte block read up to 248 bytes past it, past the end
+		 * of the file for the last track). Such a track then differs from the
+		 * core's (same_layout) and is written from the core's copy. */
+		if (0x18 + 8L * spt > size) spt = (int)((size - 0x18) / 8);
 		t->spt = spt;
 		for (j = 0; j < spt; j++) {
 			const unsigned char *id = th + 0x18 + 8 * j;
